1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,060
Last lecture ended on a real PRD line that couldn't fail a build if it tried.

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:06,528
Here's what replaces it.

3
00:00:06,528 --> 00:00:11,666
An evaluation threshold has four parts, always: what you're testing it against, what

4
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:16,560
counts as good, what fraction has to clear that bar, and what's never allowed to

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:17,599
happen even once.

6
00:00:17,599 --> 00:00:22,738
Get those four parts written down, specifically, and you have a sentence QA can run.

7
00:00:22,738 --> 00:00:25,980
Leave any one of them out, and you're back to hoping.

8
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:27,203
This is the rewrite.

9
00:00:27,203 --> 00:00:30,445
Watch what happens to the sentence from last lecture.

10
00:00:30,445 --> 00:00:35,583
Notice, too, what this format quietly forces you to do before you can even write it.

11
00:00:35,583 --> 00:00:40,476
You can't fill in "on the fifty-case golden set" without that golden set already

12
00:00:40,476 --> 00:00:41,027
existing.

13
00:00:41,027 --> 00:00:45,982
You can't say "score four or five on the rubric" without the rubric already being

14
00:00:45,982 --> 00:00:48,979
written down somewhere two people have agreed to.

15
00:00:48,979 --> 00:00:52,955
The sentence isn't just a better way of phrasing the requirement.

16
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:57,848
It's a forcing function that makes the real work happen before launch instead of

17
00:00:57,848 --> 00:00:58,215
after.

18
00:00:58,765 --> 00:01:01,344
Same intent, completely different sentence.

19
00:01:01,344 --> 00:01:06,141
Read the second one again and notice it has no adjectives doing the actual work.

20
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:10,338
Every word in it points at something a QA engineer could go and check.

21
00:01:10,338 --> 00:01:12,077
Fifty cases, named and fixed.

22
00:01:12,077 --> 00:01:14,116
A rubric with defined score bands.

23
00:01:14,116 --> 00:01:18,793
Ninety percent, not "most." And a hard floor of zero for the worst category, a

24
00:01:18,793 --> 00:01:23,710
factual error stated as fact, no matter how well the other ninety percent perform.

25
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:28,687
That last clause is doing more work than it looks like, because an average can hide

26
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:32,045
exactly the failure a launch review most needs to catch.

27
00:01:32,045 --> 00:01:34,684
Try the disagreement test on both sentences.

28
00:01:34,684 --> 00:01:39,601
Two reasonable people can argue for hours over whether a given answer was "helpful

29
00:01:39,601 --> 00:01:43,319
enough." Which one would you rather defend in a launch review?

30
00:01:43,319 --> 00:01:48,356
Almost nobody argues over whether a response scored a four against a written rubric,

31
00:01:48,356 --> 00:01:51,354
or whether it was response forty-one out of fifty.

32
00:01:51,354 --> 00:01:56,211
The second sentence doesn't just describe the bar more precisely, it changes what

33
00:01:56,211 --> 00:02:01,068
kind of conversation happens when someone questions whether the feature is ready.

34
00:02:01,618 --> 00:02:05,790
Here's why this lecture comes before the other three, not after them.

35
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:10,688
That one sentence is built from four things you don't have yet, and each one gets

36
00:02:10,688 --> 00:02:14,860
its own lecture because each one is a real decision, not a formality.

37
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,306
The dataset decides what you're actually testing against.

38
00:02:18,306 --> 00:02:23,264
The rubric decides what "good" means precisely enough for two people to agree on a

39
00:02:23,264 --> 00:02:23,627
score.

40
00:02:23,627 --> 00:02:27,497
The threshold decides how much slack you're willing to tolerate.

41
00:02:27,497 --> 00:02:32,515
And the failure floor catches the one thing an average will always hide: a rare but

42
00:02:32,515 --> 00:02:36,687
severe failure sitting quietly inside an otherwise healthy pass rate.

43
00:02:36,687 --> 00:02:41,766
Miss any of the four and the sentence quietly degrades back toward the one from last

44
00:02:41,766 --> 00:02:42,250
lecture.

45
00:02:42,250 --> 00:02:47,027
Skip the dataset and you're testing against whatever cases happened to be lying

46
00:02:47,027 --> 00:02:47,450
around.

47
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:52,468
Skip the rubric and "score four or five" means whatever the person scoring it feels

48
00:02:52,468 --> 00:02:53,012
that day.

49
00:02:53,012 --> 00:02:58,031
Skip the floor and a rare, severe failure hides inside a pass rate everyone's happy

50
00:02:58,031 --> 00:02:58,333
with.

51
00:02:58,333 --> 00:03:03,110
The sentence only works when all four clauses are load-bearing, not decorative.

52
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:06,580
Remember the seven archetypes from Section one.

53
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:10,557
Here's where that chart turns into an actual number in your PRD.

54
00:03:10,557 --> 00:03:15,715
A summariser that's wrong four times in twenty is annoying and recoverable, a human

55
00:03:15,715 --> 00:03:17,206
reads the source anyway.

56
00:03:17,206 --> 00:03:22,177
An agent that acts on the user's behalf at that same failure rate is a different

57
00:03:22,177 --> 00:03:26,589
conversation entirely, because there's no draft step catching it first.

58
00:03:26,589 --> 00:03:31,622
The threshold climbs with how directly the output reaches the user and how hard a

59
00:03:31,622 --> 00:03:33,113
wrong answer is to undo.

60
00:03:33,113 --> 00:03:36,158
Don't copy these exact numbers into your own PRD.

61
00:03:36,158 --> 00:03:40,942
Copy the habit: name the archetype first, then set the bar it actually earns,

62
00:03:40,942 --> 00:03:45,540
instead of reaching for whatever threshold felt reasonable in the meeting.

63
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:50,573
This is also where the disqualification checklist from Section one earns its keep

64
00:03:50,573 --> 00:03:50,946
again.

65
00:03:50,946 --> 00:03:55,979
If naming the archetype and its blast radius tells you the honest threshold would

66
00:03:55,979 --> 00:04:01,199
need to sit above ninety-nine percent just to be responsible, that's not a number to

67
00:04:01,199 --> 00:04:02,131
negotiate down.

68
00:04:02,131 --> 00:04:07,350
That's the checklist telling you, again, that the feature as scoped might not be the

69
00:04:07,350 --> 00:04:08,096
one to ship.

70
00:04:08,646 --> 00:04:13,375
Say the honest caveat, because the team that's just been burned by a vague criterion

71
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:15,796
often swings straight to an impossible one.

72
00:04:15,796 --> 00:04:20,300
A hundred percent sounds like the safe answer in a room full of people who don't

73
00:04:20,300 --> 00:04:21,651
want to be blamed later.

74
00:04:21,651 --> 00:04:22,158
It isn't.

75
00:04:22,158 --> 00:04:26,662
It's a threshold nothing will ever clear, which means the real decision, ship or

76
00:04:26,662 --> 00:04:31,222
don't, still gets made informally, on launch day, by whoever's most persuasive in

77
00:04:31,222 --> 00:04:31,729
the room.

78
00:04:31,729 --> 00:04:36,402
A threshold's whole job is to make that decision in advance, on paper, where it can

79
00:04:36,402 --> 00:04:37,697
be argued about calmly.

80
00:04:37,697 --> 00:04:42,032
You'll know a threshold is doing its job when it's specific enough to lose an

81
00:04:42,032 --> 00:04:42,989
argument against.

82
00:04:42,989 --> 00:04:47,718
If everyone in the room would still agree to ship at ninety percent but the document

83
00:04:47,718 --> 00:04:52,222
says ninety-nine, the number wasn't a decision, it was a performance of caution.

84
00:04:52,222 --> 00:04:56,839
Set it where you'd actually hold the line, not where it sounds most responsible in

85
00:04:56,839 --> 00:04:57,515
the meeting.

86
00:04:58,065 --> 00:05:03,118
That's the definition worth keeping for the rest of this section, and notice it's a

87
00:05:03,118 --> 00:05:07,319
checklist as much as a definition: dataset, rubric, threshold, floor.

88
00:05:07,319 --> 00:05:10,729
The exercise carries forward directly from last lecture.

89
00:05:10,729 --> 00:05:15,721
Take that same untestable line and force it into these four parts, even if some of

90
00:05:15,721 --> 00:05:18,339
the numbers are placeholders you'll revise.

91
00:05:18,339 --> 00:05:23,028
A rough threshold you can argue with beats a fluent sentence nobody can test.

92
00:05:23,028 --> 00:05:27,777
Expect the first attempt to feel uncomfortably specific, maybe even arbitrary.

93
00:05:27,777 --> 00:05:29,542
That discomfort is the point.

94
00:05:29,542 --> 00:05:34,596
A vague criterion never felt uncomfortable because it never committed to anything a

95
00:05:34,596 --> 00:05:36,301
reviewer could push back on.

96
00:05:36,301 --> 00:05:41,171
The moment your threshold makes someone in the room say "wait, is ninety percent

97
00:05:41,171 --> 00:05:44,885
actually enough here," the document is finally doing its job.

98
00:05:45,435 --> 00:05:47,206
One thing before you move on.

99
00:05:47,206 --> 00:05:50,807
Rewrite one real acceptance criterion using all four parts.

100
00:05:50,807 --> 00:05:55,874
Guess at the numbers if you have to, the structure matters more than precision on a

101
00:05:55,874 --> 00:05:59,963
first pass, and do the floor first, before the headline percentage.

102
00:05:59,963 --> 00:06:04,847
Deciding what you'll never tolerate is a clearer conversation than deciding what

103
00:06:04,847 --> 00:06:09,669
counts as usually good enough, and it tends to make the second number easier to

104
00:06:09,669 --> 00:06:10,463
agree on too.

105
00:06:10,463 --> 00:06:15,407
Next lecture builds the first of those four parts properly: specifying the golden

106
00:06:15,407 --> 00:06:18,581
dataset your threshold actually gets tested against.
